  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1120 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nThis evening's upper air analysis features a mid-level ridge over\nthe Southeast United States and Tennessee Valley with an upper-\nlevel low located over New Mexico. This is resulting in southwest\nflow aloft occurring across the Lower Mississippi Valley tonight.\nDry and warm conditions are prevailing this evening with\ntemperatures ranging from the upper 60s to lower 70s near the\nTennessee River, and upper 70s to around 80 near the Mississippi\nRiver.\n\nThe aforementioned ridge to our east will bring another hot and\nmainly dry day to the Mid-South with afternoon highs in the lower\n90s across many locations. This prolonged period of hot and\nmainly dry weather is about to end as a weak cold front will\nbegin to approach the area beginning late Thursday night and\ncontinuing into the weekend.\n\nThe upper-level low currently over New Mexico is expected to\nweaken into a positively-tilted  open wave trough as it moves\ninto portions of the Central and Southern Plains on Thursday.\nShowers and a few thunderstorms will begin to spread into\nportions of northeast Arkansas towards Friday morning as lift\noccurs within the right entrance region of a departing northern\nstream jet streak. The front is expected to stall out south of I-\n40 across the area on Friday with best upper-level support\ndeparting the region. Weak shear overall (<25 kts) combined with\nmarginal instability is anticipated to mitigate the potential for\nany severe weather. \n\nLatest deterministic and ensemble solutions indicate a second \nwave will move across the region Saturday afternoon into Saturday \nnight bringing another continued chance for showers and \nthunderstorms to areas along and south of I-40. Rainfall totals \nremain largely consistent with the previous forecast, averaging \nless than 1 inch along and north of I-40, and values between 1-1.5\ninches through the period south. This projected rainfall is not \nexpected to alleviate the substantial rainfall deficits that are \nalready occurring across the area.\n\nHigh pressure will build in behind the cold front for the latter\nhalf of the weekend resulting in cooler and seasonable\ntemperatures arriving across the Mid-South for early October with\nhighs in the 70s to lower 80s and lows by early next week as low\nas the upper 40s to lower 50s across favored locations in\nnorthwest Tennessee.",
